# /etc/sriov-mac-sync.conf - all optional

# Uplink/bridge pairs to manage. Empty means autodetect: every interface with
# virtual functions - or itself a virtual function - that ends up in a bridge,
# following bonds. Two things naming pairs by hand changes: --pair on the
# command line REPLACES this list (EXCLUDE/EXTRA merge), and the orphan sweep
# is off - a pair removed from this list keeps its filter entries until
# --flush, because only autodetection can know it sees every uplink.
#PAIRS="nic1:vmbr1 nic0:vmbr0"

# Seconds of silence before a timed pass. This is paranoia, not the mechanism.
# Addresses are registered the moment the kernel reports them and removed when
# they move out onto the wire or their bridge port goes - an aged-out address
# whose port still hangs in the bridge is kept, see the README. Interface
# changes - a new NIC, a new bridge - arrive as link notifications. A VF address changed silently (a down PF, a
# guest-side change over the driver mailbox) is caught by the fresh driver
# question that precedes any filter growth. A learn that names a port or a
# bridge the daemon does not know makes it re-read the picture by itself.
# The timed pass exists for a host that is silent AND wrong, and it warns
# when it finds work - that is what --resync is for by hand.
#RESYNC=3600

# The filter capacity to respect: warn above it, and shed kept quiet
# addresses as the list nears it. The vport list on ConnectX-4 Lx holds 128;
# other hardware differs. Where the card reports its capacity through devlink
# the daemon uses that instead, and setting this overrides both.
#MAX_MACS=128

# Addresses never to register, separated by commas or whitespace.
#EXCLUDE=""

# Addresses to register unconditionally, for a device that never speaks first.
# The wire-side and own-address rules still override this.
#EXTRA=""
